## Runtime Layout

ParticleOS initializes a checksummed PostgreSQL 18 cluster in
`/var/lib/pgsql`, disables its TCP listeners, creates the unprivileged
`stalwart` database role, and authenticates the matching OS account through
peer authentication on `/run/postgresql`. There is no database password or environment file.

PostgreSQL and the host integration are part of the OS. The executable, allocator libraries, and WebUI run from the signed, release-pinned
service image selected at `/var/lib/particleos/stalwart/current.raw`; they are
not installed in the host `/usr`. The DDI is EROFS with embedded dm-verity
metadata and an OBS-project root-hash signature verified by PID 1.

## Initial Provisioning

A fresh registry starts with `STALWART_RECOVERY_MODE=1`. Stalwart writes a
random temporary `admin` credential to the protected service journal:

```sh
run0 journalctl -u stalwart.service
```

1. Connect through the console or an SSH tunnel to `localhost:8080`. The
   public firewall never admits port 8080.
2. Create the permanent administrator.
3. Configure the production hostname, TLS certificates, Ed25519 DKIM keys,
   domains, relay policy, and abuse controls before accepting mail.
4. Restart Stalwart. A registry with a non-expired password-bearing
   administrator selects the normal protocol profile; an unprovisioned
   registry remains in recovery mode.

## Network and DNS

The public firewall admits only TCP 25, 443, 465, and 993. POP3, ManageSieve,
plaintext client protocols, PostgreSQL, and recovery TCP 8080 remain closed.
Stalwart may create external connections only to SMTP TCP 25 and HTTPS TCP
443.

All DNS traffic stays on loopback and passes through `systemd-resolved`.
Stalwart uses the `127.0.0.54:53` TCP proxy so DNS messages cross the host's
authenticated Cloudflare DNS-over-TLS path while the records required for DANE
remain available to Stalwart's own DNSSEC validation. Blocked DoT or failed
validation fails closed.

The boot gate checks only the local resolver listener and a bounded localhost
transaction. It never queries an Internet name, so a provider, network, or
Cloudflare outage cannot consume an otherwise healthy counted OS boot attempt.
Normal mail retry semantics handle those runtime outages.

## Health Gate

The health gate runs on every counted OS boot and after every application-image
activation. Recovery mode checks the database and loopback WebUI. Normal mode
performs bounded SMTP, SMTPS, IMAPS, and HTTPS exchanges over IPv4 and IPv6,
validates TLS certificates, checks browser security headers, and compares the
served WebUI with the digest generated from the mounted image.

Stable exit codes identify the failed layer:

| Exit code | Failure |
|---:|---|
| 10 | Listener absent |
| 20 | TLS handshake |
| 30 | Certificate validation |
| 40 | Protocol exchange |
| 50 | Datastore |
| 60 | WebUI mismatch |

Migration errors leave Stalwart failed instead of inactive-successful, so
systemd restart and health handling remain effective.

## WebUI Integrity

The WebUI is checksum-pinned inside the signed service image. Stalwart accepts
only `file:///usr/share/stalwart/webui.zip` and reloads it on every process
start instead of consulting a mutable application cache. The executable and
WebUI are therefore atomic with the selected Stalwart image, independently of
the OS A/B slot.

## Storage and Recovery

Application data lives in the local PostgreSQL cluster. Continuous WAL
archiving, verified base backups, retention rules, and point-in-time recovery
are documented in [`BACKUP-RECOVERY.md`](./BACKUP-RECOVERY.md). PITR remains
disabled until an operator mounts independent encrypted backup storage.

Signed application-image acquisition, compatibility metadata, atomic
selection, and rollback are documented in
[`IMAGE-UPDATES.md`](./IMAGE-UPDATES.md).

## Diagnostics

The `particleos_stalwart` SELinux policy limits Stalwart to labelled
configuration, state, log, runtime, and temporary paths; the PostgreSQL Unix
socket; the system resolver; and the selected SMTP, IMAP, and HTTP port types.

```sh
run0 systemctl status postgresql.service particleos-stalwart-database.service stalwart.service
run0 systemctl start particleos-mailserver-health.service
run0 journalctl -u particleos-mailserver-health.service
run0 -u stalwart psql --host=/run/postgresql --dbname=stalwart --command='SELECT 1'
```

Changing the database location, public protocols, or recovery-listener policy
requires a signed OS change covering nftables, SELinux, credentials, service
ordering, health checks, and this operational contract.
